## Section 3: UserModel (`lib/userModel.ts`)

The functions change signature to accept `certId` and operate on the per-cert slice:

```typescript
// Initialize a fresh cert slice with all domains at 50
function initCertProgress(certId: CertId): CertProgress {
  const domains = CERT_REGISTRY[certId].domains;
  const domainScores = Object.fromEntries(domains.map(d => [d.id, 50]));
  return { domainScores, sessions: [], seenCombinations: [] };
}

// Read — creates the cert slice if it doesn't exist yet
export function readUserModel(userId: string): UserModel

// Get the cert progress slice, initializing if needed
export function getCertProgress(userModel: UserModel, certId: CertId): CertProgress

// Update — writes back into the correct cert slice
export function updateUserModel(userId: string, certId: CertId, session: SessionResult): void

// Pick weakest domain within a cert slice
export function pickWeakestDomain(certProgress: CertProgress): string
```

`debriefAgent` currently calls `updateUserModel` internally. It will need `certId` passed in — sourced from `scenario.cert`.

`app/page.tsx` initializes `userModel` from sessionStorage; the shape change means old sessionStorage data is incompatible. A version guard (`userModel.certs` exists → v2, else discard and reinitialize) handles stale data cleanly.

